In article <123100016@uxa.cso.uiuc.edu> sapg0386@uxa.cso.uiuc.edu writes:

>Nevertheless
>the documentation should tell me everything I could discover from a few
>hours of eye-balling the source code which maybe is what they assume 
>everyone will do anyway.  There should be a complete list of
>monsters, spells, wands, items, and such.   

I disagree somewhat.  While most of us have used spoilers of some sort
or another, part of the fun of the game lies in the fact that the
complete facts are not spelled out for you, that you can gain
knowledge through rumors, oracularities, experience, talking to
others, so forth.  Most magical items, once identified, have names
which make it fairly clear what they do.

>And a general description of
>the ground rules.  For example I had a level 9 priest who still couldn't
>read spells without dipping them in holy water first--and even then he couldn't
>cast them.  It is a sort of false advertising to have all these spell books
>laying around when they will never be of any use.  Empire does this sort
>of thing right by describing the algorithms they use.  Hack should tell
>you if a high IQ helps with magic and if a high wisdom does not.

This is true.  Doesn't it tell you?  I forget.

>Another grip is this
>luck stuff.  Having it is ok, so long as it isn't secret.  A hidden
>parameter just makes the game a pain; if they were on the status line
>then at least you could learn from your mistakes.

This, to me, is also part of the flavor of the game.  If luck (same goes
for how much your god likes you) were on the status line, it would
just become another boring stat you had to keep track of.  Definitely
keep it a hidden variable.  Maybe luck should be displayed when you
quaff a potion of, uh..., you know.  The one which shows you what
intrinsics you have and how you are aligned....  How about showing these
facts (intrinsics, luck, etc.) when you end you game, in the same
spirit of showing you your inventory when you die?

